-- Passage rg8-authors-purpose-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2ebb0f9f-ee5f-5ae6-a7eb-5b7935049ede', 'rg8-authors-purpose-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Give the Lot to the Gardeners$q$, $q$For six years, the lot at the corner of Fenwick Avenue and Third Street has held nothing but weeds and broken glass. This spring, the town council will decide its future. Some council members want to pave it for twenty parking spaces. I believe the lot should become a community garden instead.

A garden would give neighbors something that parking spaces cannot. Families in the apartment buildings nearby have no yards. A garden would let them grow vegetables, meet each other, and teach children where food comes from. Gardens also soak up rainwater, while pavement sends it rushing into streets that already flood after storms.

Shop owners on Third Street argue that customers cannot find parking, and they say fewer customers means fewer jobs. That concern is fair. On busy Saturdays, I have watched drivers circle the block three times. However, the town parking garage two blocks away is rarely more than half full. A clear sign pointing drivers to the garage would solve much of the problem for far less money than paving a lot.

Others worry that a garden will be messy or abandoned after one season. The Fenwick Neighbors Group has already collected forty signatures from people who promise to tend plots, and the high school science club has offered to manage a compost bin.

The council should choose the option that serves the most people for the longest time. Twenty parking spaces help a few drivers for a few minutes. A garden can feed a neighborhood for years.$q$, 'draft')
