-- Passage rg8-authors-purpose-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d79764bc-b4ac-50e2-906d-ae2787ea5986', 'rg8-authors-purpose-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Counting Birds Counts$q$, $q$Every winter, thousands of volunteers across the country spend a day counting birds. They stand in parks, look out kitchen windows, and walk along frozen creeks with notebooks in hand. Their tallies are sent to researchers who track how bird populations change over time. In my view, these volunteer counts are among the most valuable tools in modern science.

No team of professional scientists could cover as much ground. A single researcher might survey a few sites in a season. Volunteers can watch tens of thousands of sites on the same day. That kind of coverage helps scientists notice when a species begins to disappear from one region or appear in a new one.

Some researchers question whether amateur data can be trusted. A beginner might confuse two similar sparrows or count the same flock twice. These are real mistakes, and they do happen. Yet the people who organize the counts have built in ways to catch them. Volunteers receive identification guides and short training videos. Unusual sightings must include a photo or a second observer, and experts review every surprising report before it enters the database.

Large numbers also help. When thousands of people report the same pattern, a few errors do not change the overall picture.

Anyone who enjoys the outdoors can join a count. You do not need expensive gear, only patience and a willingness to learn. Science needs more eyes, and yours could help.$q$, 'draft')
