-- Passage rg8-authors-purpose-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c3d222cd-bdb6-58d9-b2ba-9ccaec2f8645', 'rg8-authors-purpose-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$One Sport or Many?$q$, $q$By age eleven, many young athletes are told to pick one sport and train for it all year. Coaches and parents hope that early focus will lead to a spot on a high school team or even a college scholarship. I understand that hope, but I believe most young athletes are better off playing several sports until high school.

Playing different sports builds different skills. Soccer trains footwork and endurance. Basketball sharpens quick decisions. Swimming strengthens the whole body without pounding the joints. An athlete who does all three develops a wider base of movement, and that base often makes her better at whichever sport she eventually chooses.

Repeating the same motions all year can also wear the body down. Doctors who treat young athletes report that overuse injuries, such as sore elbows in pitchers and aching knees in runners, are common among kids who play one sport in every season.

Supporters of early focus point out that skill takes practice, and a player who trains year-round will log more hours. That is true. A twelve-year-old who plays only tennis will probably beat a twelve-year-old who splits time among three sports. But winning at twelve is not the goal. Many athletes who specialize early burn out and quit by fifteen, while athletes who play several sports often keep playing longer because the variety keeps the game fun.

Young athletes have years to choose a single path. They should spend their early years exploring.$q$, 'draft')
