-- Passage rg8-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7f6ebf51-da34-5449-9df9-a01fa095b7a1', 'rg8-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Pen Versus Keyboard$q$, $q$When my school gave every eighth grader a laptop, most of my classmates stopped carrying notebooks. Typing notes seemed faster and neater, and nobody had to decode messy handwriting later. After a semester of trying both methods, though, I have gone back to paper for most of my classes.

The biggest reason is how I listen. When I type, I can keep up with almost every word my teacher says, so I copy sentences without thinking about them. When I write by hand, I cannot possibly keep up. I am forced to choose what matters and put it in my own words. That choice is where the learning happens. Several studies of college students have found something similar: students who took notes by hand often understood the ideas better on later tests.

Laptops also bring distractions. A notification or an open tab can pull my attention away in seconds. A notebook has no messages.

To be fair, typed notes have clear advantages. They are easy to search, easy to share with a classmate who was absent, and impossible to lose if they are saved online. Students with hand injuries or certain learning differences may need a keyboard to take notes at all.

So I am not arguing that laptops should be banned. My point is simpler. Students who use laptops out of habit should try a notebook for a few weeks. They may find, as I did, that slower notes lead to deeper understanding.$q$, 'draft')
