-- Passage rg8-authors-purpose-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('52c5b03d-8bf1-5168-bbee-98d41c30d908', 'rg8-authors-purpose-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Bridge at Millbrook$q$, $q$In 1887, the farming town of Millbrook faced a difficult choice. The only way across the Tallow River was a ferry that ran when the water was calm. In spring floods, the ferry stopped for weeks, and farmers on the east bank could not reach the market or the mill. A group of residents proposed building a covered wooden bridge.

The plan divided the town. Supporters argued that a bridge would let farmers sell crops all year and would bring travelers who spent money in local shops. Opponents had serious objections. The bridge would cost more than the town collected in taxes in three years. The ferry operator, Elias Brandt, would lose his living. Some families also feared that a road over the river would bring strangers and change the quiet character of the town.

After months of meetings, the town voted to build the bridge, but it also agreed to hire Brandt as the bridge's first toll keeper. Construction took two years.

Historians still disagree about the decision. Town records show that Millbrook's market grew by nearly half in the ten years after the bridge opened. Yet the same records show that the town borrowed heavily and did not finish repaying the debt until 1912.

Looking at the evidence, I believe the bridge was worth its cost. The debt was painful, but it was temporary. The ability to cross the river in every season shaped the town for more than a century, and the bridge still carries foot traffic today.$q$, 'draft')
