-- Passage rg8-authors-purpose-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0df2b23f-b3fc-5e43-9638-023cf1e77b6a', 'rg8-authors-purpose-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Should Beavers Return to Clearwater Valley?$q$, $q$For nearly a century, no beavers have lived in Clearwater Valley. Now a group of wildlife scientists has proposed bringing a small number back. The plan has sparked a lively debate, and both sides raise points worth understanding.

Supporters see beavers as natural engineers. When beavers build dams, they create ponds and wetlands that store water during dry summers. These wetlands give fish, frogs, and birds new places to live. Some scientists believe that beaver ponds can even slow the spread of wildfires, because wet ground and green plants do not burn easily.

Many farmers in the valley are less enthusiastic. Beaver dams can flood fields, block irrigation ditches, and drown trees that landowners want to keep. One farmer, Rosa Delgado, has said that she already struggles to keep water flowing to her crops and does not want an animal that changes the river overnight. Road crews add another concern: beaver ponds sometimes cover drainage pipes under roads and wash the roads out.

The scientists have responded with a few proposals. They would release beavers only in upper parts of the valley, far from most farms. They would also install devices called flow pipes, which let water pass through a dam so that ponds cannot rise too high. Farmers would be able to call a wildlife office if a dam caused problems.

Whether these steps are enough remains an open question. The valley's residents will discuss the plan at public meetings this spring before any decision is made.$q$, 'draft')
